# Line endings, and why this file is not optional on Windows.
#
# `core.autocrlf=true` is the default on a Windows install: git stores LF and
# hands the working tree CRLF. For source that is invisible - the compiler does
# not care. For **migrations** it is a live fault.
#
# `sqlx::migrate!` embeds each migration by hashing the bytes it finds in the
# working tree, and every database records the hash of what was applied to it.
# So a file that git rewrote on checkout - which happens on a merge, a branch
# switch, a fresh clone - gets a different hash from the one already in
# `_sqlx_migrations`, and every boot afterwards fails with:
#
#     migration 1 was previously applied but has been modified
#
# Nothing was modified. The bytes on disk simply stopped matching the bytes git
# stores. That cost a broken tenant sweep once; this file is what stops it
# recurring, by making the working tree identical to the object store.
#
# `text=auto eol=lf` also keeps the repository readable from Linux, which is
# where it is deployed - see `[[phonix-runs-on-a-vps-at-evrykit]]`.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Stated again for the files whose bytes are a contract, so that anybody
# loosening the rule above has to walk past the reason.
*.sql   text eol=lf
*.rs    text eol=lf
*.toml  text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf

# Windows-native scripts are read by cmd.exe and PowerShell, which want CRLF.
*.bat   text eol=crlf
*.cmd   text eol=crlf
*.ps1   text eol=crlf

# Never touched. A "line ending" in a PNG is a corrupted PNG.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.webp  binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.wasm  binary
